0,Dhauwurd Wurrung language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhauwurd_Wurrung_language,"March 11, 2026 - Gunditj Wurrung, meaning ""Gunditj language"" is used by a contemporary teacher of the language, Gunditjmara musician Corey Theatre. Keerray Woorroong (Girai Wurrung, Kirrae wuurong, Kiriwurrung, etc.) is regarded by the Victorian Aboriginal Corporation for Languages (following Clark) as a separate ...",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
1,Girai wurrung - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Girai_wurrung,"The Girai wurrung, also spelt Kirrae Wuurong and Kirrae Whurrung, are an Aboriginal Australian people who traditionally occupied the territory between Mount Emu Creek and the Hopkins River up to Mount Hamilton, and the Western Otways from the Gellibrand River to the Hopkins River, in what is now the state of Victoria.",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
2,Gunditjmara - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunditjmara,"April 7, 2026 - Keerray Woorroong (Girai Wurrung, Kirrae wuurong, Kiriwurrung, etc.) is regarded by the Victorian Aboriginal Corporation for Languages as a separate language; it is of the Girai wurrung people.",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
3,Keerray-Woorroong | Indigenous Languages,https://indigenous.sl.nsw.gov.au/communities/keerray-woorroong,Home Communities Keerray-Woorroong · Show on Map · Get Involved! 8/07/2017 · Reclaiming Indigenous languages: Our transcribe-a-thon for NAIDOC Week ·,Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
4,Keerray Woorroong,https://aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/S25,"Krishna-Pillay says that, historically, there was no general name for this language (p. 17) and discusses the various labels that have been used, including Gunditjmara (p.18). The Victorian Aboriginal Corporation for Languages follows Clark in treating Keerray Woorroong as a separate language.",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
5,Keerray Woorrong - Howitt & Fison's Archive,https://howittandfison.org/culturalgroups/15527,AIATSIS code: S25 AIATSIS reference name: Keerray Woorroong^ See: https://collection.aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/s25,Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
6,"Book, Victorian Aboriginal Corporation for Languages et al, Yarnin up : Keerray Woorroong, 2010",https://victoriancollections.net.au/items/56c69094400d0c3518d14c6d,"March 3, 2017 - Words and phrases in Keerray Woorroong. maps, word lists, colour illustrations",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
7,"ngulu wurneet, galada-al wurrung-u, parniwaru tyalingi, waran woorroong-ee, barringgi dyaling | river of language | About us | Bunjilaka",https://museumsvictoria.com.au/bunjilaka/about-us/birrarung-gallery/river-of-language/,"The result was the multimedia soundscape exhibition ngulu wurneet, galada-al wurrung-u, parniwaru tyalingi, waran woorroong-ee, barringgi dyaling | River of Language. The exhibition was on show in the Birrarung Gallery at Bunjilaka Aboriginal Cultural Centre, Melbourne Museum from June 2019 to October 2020. The co-curators and artists, Vicki Couzens Keerray Wooroong and Brendan Kennedy Tati Tati Wadi Wadi Mutti Mutti created the exhibition in partnership with VACL and Museums Victoria.",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
8,Showing 7 items matching keerray woorroong,https://victoriancollections.net.au/collections?q=keerray+woorroong,"Words and phrases in Keerray Woorroong.maps, word lists, colour illustrationskeerray woorroong, pronunciation, place names, kinship terms, feelings, body parts, country, animals, weather, counting ... ... ...Keerray Woorroong...Victorian Aboriginal Corporation for Languages 33 Saxon Street Brunswick melbourne Bangerang Bidawal Boonwurrung Dharwurd Wurrung Dja Dja Wurrung Djab Wurrung Gunnai Kurnai Jardwadjali Jadawadjali Keerray Woorroong Latji Latji Peek Wurrong Taungurung Wathaurung Wergaia Wotjobaluk Wurundjeri Yorta Yorta maps, b&w illustrations, colour illustrations, colour photographs Meerreeng-an here is my country : the story of Aboriginal Victoria told through art Book Chris Keeler Vicki Couzens ...",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
9,Keerraywoorroong - Victorian Aboriginal Corporation for Languages,https://vacl.org.au/language/keerraywoorroong/,"Meaning: ‘bear language’, a reference to the concentration of koalas in this forested region (Dawson 1881).",Keerray-Woorroong,Australian
